course: dictionary
lesson: 9660
title: Subscriptions and free trials
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A subscription is money you pay regularly, often every month or every year, to keep getting something, like a magazine, a music app or a club. «A subscription is paying regularly, often every month, for something like music, videos or a game.»
explain: Look at the words around it for clues. «My subscription ends soon» means the paying arrangement will stop soon. Try your meaning in the sentence: "My money-paying arrangement ends soon" still makes sense, so you have the right idea.
explain: A free trial lets you try something for nothing for a short time. «A free trial turns into a paid subscription unless you cancel it in time.» If nobody cancels, the payments start, so ask an adult before you sign up.
explain: Small amounts add up. Leo forgot a cartoon app, so £5.99 left Dad's account every month, and £11.98 went out in two months for an app nobody opened.
fact: A subscription means paying regularly, often every month or every year.
fact: «Subscription» is a noun: a subscription, subscriptions.
fact: To stop paying, you cancel the subscription.
fact: A free trial often turns into a paid subscription if nobody cancels it.
fact: Small monthly subscriptions add up to big amounts over a year, so check them and cancel any you do not use.
exercise: «Free music apps earn money from adverts; a paid subscription removes them.» What is a paid subscription? | A type of advert on the radio | A free game you can play with friends | A card you use at the shop | *Money you pay regularly to get something
exercise: Dad pays £5.99 every month so Leo can watch cartoons. What is this called? | *A subscription | A souvenir | A subtraction | A submarine
exercise: «A free trial turns into a paid subscription unless you cancel it in time.» What must you do to stop the payments starting? | *Cancel it in time | Share the account with a friend | Pay for it early | Wait until the trial is over and forget it
test: «Small monthly subscriptions add up to big amounts over a year.» What does add up mean here? | Stop suddenly | *Grow into a big total | Go up a hill | Do maths sums only in class
test: Nadia says a subscription and a free trial are the same. Why is she wrong? | *A free trial costs nothing at first, but a subscription is paid regularly | They mean exactly the same | Subscriptions are always free | Trials happen only at school
exam: What is the best first step before you sign up for a free trial? | Enter your card details quickly | *Ask an adult first | Share it with all your friends | Start paying for the full year
